course: dictionary
lesson: 15156
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Scoot is a verb, which means a doing word. Its forms are scoot, scoots, scooted and scooting. Many everyday words have more than one meaning, so look at the words around them to find the one that fits.
explain: «I'd better scoot, or I'll be late for tea.» Here scoot means leave quickly. The clues are I'd better and late: the child is going somewhere, not riding anything.
explain: «Can you scoot over so I can sit down?» Here scoot over means move a little to make room. Scoot can also mean ride a scooter, pushing along with one foot, as in «Ella scooted to school on her new scooter.» Try each meaning in the sentence and see which one makes sense.
fact: Scoot is a verb: scoot, scoots, scooted, scooting.
fact: «Scoot» means to go somewhere quickly or leave quickly.
fact: «Scoot» can also mean to ride a scooter, pushing with one foot.
fact: «Scoot over» means to move a little to make room. This use is mostly found in US informal talk.
fact: Scoot is informal, so you will hear it in friendly talk more often than in formal writing.
exercise: «I'd better scoot, or I'll be late for tea.» What does scoot mean here? | To cook a meal | *To leave quickly | To sing loudly | To fall asleep
exercise: «Ella scooted to school on her new scooter.» How did Ella travel? | She swam across the river | She went by bus | She ran in the rain | *She rode a scooter, pushing with one foot
exercise: «Can you scoot over so I can sit down?» What does scoot over mean in this sentence? | *Move a little to make room | Run away from the bench | Fall off the bench | Sing a song
test: «The puppy scooted off after the ball.» What does scooted mean here? | Barked loudly | *Ran off quickly | Sat down slowly | Slept under the table
test: Dad says, «Scoot along, or we'll miss the bus.» What does scoot along mean? | *Hurry along | Sing a song | Stop and rest | Sit down
exam: Which sentence uses scoot to mean ride a scooter? | *Sam likes to scoot around the park on his new scooter. | I need to scoot off to bed. | Please scoot over, Sam. | Scoot is a verb.
